                    text: "How a company charges decides how lumpy its results are. **Licence** sellers book a large fee when a bank signs and then earn annual maintenance: Newgen's Indian and Middle Eastern customers buy this way, which is why its Q4 margin was 34% and its Q1 margin 16%, and why Nucleus and Intellect report big quarterly swings. **Subscription** sellers bill every month or year: 94.5% of Capillary's Q1 revenue was subscription, Newgen's SaaS revenue grew 40% in Q1, and Amagi's customers expand their spending by about a quarter a year (net revenue retention of 125–127%). **Transaction** sellers are paid per use: Zaggle earns a share of card interchange, NPST earns per UPI payment processed, Protean per PAN card issued, and Unicommerce per order shipped. Subscription and transaction models are steadier, but only if the company discloses what is recurring, and most here do not. My view: in this group, reward disclosure; the companies that publish ARR and retention have been the ones whose guidance held.",
